re was  pretty simple a few years ago all you  could do with druid was basically  you would have your data your static set  of data in a distributed file system  somewhere  you would run a mapreduce job over that  data create the segment structure that  druid understands  and load it across a set of druid nodes  called historical nodes  and historical nodes are pretty simple  they know how to load data  drop data and scan data really quickly  in front of the historical nodes we have  another set of nodes called broker nodes  and broker nodes encapsulate query  scatter gather functionality  so when you send a query to a broker  node it knows what pieces of data live  where  it forwards the query down the  historical nodes compute their portion  of the answer in parallel  return results the broker node the  broker merges results and returns to the  caller  so we deployed into production a few  years ago  and what we gained with using druid was  we felt we had solved our query latency  problem  druid gave us arbitrary data exploration  and it gave us very fast queries  some benchmarks or some results from our  druid cluster as of a few months ago  um on data sets that are over 100  terabytes in size  the vast majority of our queries return  in an order of milliseconds  so we introduced druid but there were  still a bunch of problems  that were still remaining in the first  version of druid we had to load data  versus  in this mapreduce process and batch  loading data was very slow  we still didn't fully have this concept  of real time  we could not explore events as they were  occurring and if we were able to do that  there were a lot of features that we  could unlock such as alerts  we could do operational monitoring and  so forth  so to talk a little bit more about how  to build  a real-time data pipeline i'm actually  going to hand things off to jion  who's going to go into a little bit more  detail  hey uh cool i think the mic works  um so like feng jin said  we sort of in the early days realized  that would make sense is to separate  data processing and data storage from  data querying  and so the data querying system that we  ended up using was druid  and like fangshin said for data  processing in the early days we used  hadoop  mapreduce which worked but it had some  issues  uh so right so it looks sort of like  this hadoop for pre-processing and  storage and druid for queries  um the way that we did things back in  those days which was maybe  two years ago is our clients uploaded  data to s3  uh we're all running our service all  runs in the amazon cloud  um and we use hadoop and pig to clean it  up transform it and join it  and by joining it um i mean that our  data is mostly ad tech data so we have  impressions and clicks and we want to  correlate those to figure out things  like click through rate um  so after running those mapreduce jobs we  load the result into druid  and the typical turnaround time for that  whole process is  two to eight hours after the event  occurs and from that point it's  available for  the sort of arbitrary slice and dice  analytics changing was talking about  um what we started doing last year was  building a real-time version of this  pipeline  um so we decided to go  go back to what the bad platform  actually does and figure out how we can  make it real time  so there's there's three things that a  data pipeline does you have to acquire  raw data somehow  you have to process that data the  transforming and cleaning and joining  and you have to load the process data  into your query engine  which in our case is druid  um so we attack each of these  three things independently the first  thing  uh acquiring raw data we use kafka for  for those not familiar with kafka it's  an open source  product from linkedin  it's uh it's sort of a big message queue  in the sky  uh you have messages coming in messages  going out and it buffers them up for you  it's very high throughput it's got a  straightforward design which was the  the reason that we chose it over the  other things out there  and what we did was we put an http api  in front of it  so our customers can post the stuff over  the web  the architecture of a kafka system looks  something like this  you have kafka brokers sitting in the  middle you have producers on the left  which are things that generate events  and you have consumers on the right  which are things that process events  the nice thing about this is that the  producers and consumers don't have to  know about each other  the producers put things into kafka and  the consumers take things out  and if one of them is having issues then  as long as the kafka brokers in the  middle are working which they usually  are everything is fine  um the next thing to tackle was  processing events to  cleaning up transforming and joining so  for that we use storm  it's a stream processor it's also open  source it was originally developed at a  little startup called backtype and then  popularized at twitter it processes one  event at a time you set up these sort of  computation graphs where tuples come in  somewhere and then fly around and  eventually  fly out and  right so we want these storm topologies  which is what storm jobs are called to  do the same thing that our hadoop jobs  had done  so we look at what the doop jobs do they  they're mapreduce jobs they do three  things they load data from somewhere in  hadoop it's usually s3  or hdfs they map the  data which is sort of taking every event  and assigning a key and a value to it  and they reduce the events which is  taking  every thing every value with the same  key collecting it together and then  doing some sort of reduction on it which  in our case is  usually a join or an aggregation  load and map operations are pretty  streaming friendly  there's not really much to do with them  you can load one event at a time and map  one event at a time  but it's assigning a keen value to it  the reduce is the