w there are a lot of different choices  in  a query layer and we generally recommend  that when you're choosing a query layer  you try and  pick a solution that's optimized for the  types of queries you're trying to make  so for us we are particularly concerned  with  business intelligence style queries and  these queries  might be things like we want to know how  much revenue was generated over time  broken down by some demographic or  in the advertising world this could be  the top publishers  based on clicks over the last month or  how many unique visitors visited some  site broken down by some user  demographic  so in these types of queries uh what  we're not doing  is we're not dumping an entire data set  nor  are we really querying for an individual  row what we really care about  is a filtered view of some set of data  and we're primarily concerned with  performing some sort of  aggregation  in order to answer some of these  questions uh there's different solutions  that we actually tried and i'm sure many  of you have tried as well  we started out with looking at  relational databases and within this  class of solutions you have  my you have postgres many others  what we found with trying to use  relational databases  was the scan speed over your data tended  to be quite slow  so we also looked at nosql key value  stores  uh within this class of data stores you  have solutions like hbase  and like cassandra can kind of be placed  here as well  and we're trying to do like business  intelligent style queries with a lot of  aggregates  with these nosql key value stores what  you often end up doing  is precomputing results so you try and  pre-compute out  every single qr every single query that  a user can make  and what we found was the pre  computation time  was getting to be really really  expensive  um we also actually evaluated a bunch of  commercial databases  for these types of queries you know you  have your verticas you have your  redshifts and  many many other solutions but we  tended to prefer open source we believe  that  no solution is going to exactly meet  your needs  and the customization and flexibility  you get with open source is actually  really powerful  so a few years ago we actually ended up  building our own data store  and that data store is called druid  druid was first open sourced in late  2012  since then it's seen a growing community  and we've seen contributors from many  different organizations  druid now runs in production at  companies you've probably heard of such  as  yahoo or netflix and many other startups  that you probably  haven't heard of druid is  primarily designed from the ground up  for low latency ingestion and  aggregation  so druid is highly optimized for the  types of queries that we were trying to  make  and why druid is particularly good for a  certain class of queries  is really in its architecture and i just  have some examples here  imagine we have a raw data set like the  one i have showing here  this could be a sample data set from  online advertising data  you might have a timestamp dimension you  might have a bunch of attributes  and you might have a couple of metrics  in here  and one of the first things that druid  does when it ingests data  is it tries to roll the data up and what  we  mean by roll up is instead of  storing every single raw event you try  and  do a group by over the attributes or  dimensions of the data set  you try and aggregate the metrics  involved with the raw data  and you truncate the timestamps so as  opposed to storing all the raw data  you store hourly truncated data  what it further does is it partitions  data  by time time is a bit of a special  dimension in druid right now  maybe one day it won't be but in this  example here  how druid shards data is basically  it creates an immutable block of data  for a certain range of time  and this block is called a druid segment  so here we have two segments for two  separate days of data  most of the data in druid is immutable  and  immutable data confers a lot of  advantages  for one you can get reconsistency for  free  when you're dealing with immutable data  and a druid's parallelism model is such  that you use  one thread to scan one immutable segment  so if you want to do more concurrent  reads you can just add more cores  and you can get more reads that way you  can also have multiple threads  scan the same underlying data so really  druid is a system  that's really really optimized for reads  we try and keep our segments such that  computation  or scans over a given segment usually  completes in an order of milliseconds  and what that allows for is pretty  efficient resource utilization  you keep your units of computation small  and you frequently yield resources  and that's actually pretty good in a  multi-tenant environment  and finally with druid's concept of  immutable segments  uh it's a pretty simple distribution and  replication model  when you want to load balance move data  around you just actually move blocks of  data around  and when you want to replicate data you  just replicate segments you just create  blocks  some more information druid is  fundamentally a column store  and the advantages of column stores  versus row stores for doing aggregation  for doing business intelligence style  queries  is pretty well documented you only scan  and load  whatever it is you need and with most  column stores there's a lot of different  compression algorithms  that you can introduce druid also  borrows a lot of ideas  from search infrastructure so there's  unique indexes that it builds  such as inverted indexes if you're not  familiar with inverted indexes  you don't have to worry too much the  idea is that druid builds a lot of  unique indexes  that make sure that when you issue a  query only exactly  the data that's pertaining to that query  is ever scanned  so it's pretty efficient about doing  reads  in the early days of druid the  architecture was  pretty simple a few years ago all you  could do with druid was basically  you would have your data your static set  of data in a distributed file system  somewhere  you would run a mapreduce job over that  data create the segment structure that  druid understands  and load it across a set of druid nodes  called historical nodes  and historical nodes are pretty simple  they know how to load data  drop data and scan data really quickly  in front of the historical nodes we have  another set of nodes called broker nodes  and broker nodes encapsulate query  scatter gather functionality  so when you send a query to a broker  node it knows what pieces of data live  where  it forwards the query down the  historical nodes compute their portion  of the answer in parallel  return results the broker node the  broker merges results and returns to the  caller  so we deployed into production a few  years ago  and what we gained with using druid was  we felt we had solved our query latency  problem  druid gave us arbitrary data exploration  and it gave us very fast queries  some benchmarks or some results from our  druid cluster as of a few months ago  um on data sets that are over 100  terabytes in size  the vast majority of our queries return  in an order of milliseconds  so we introduced druid but there were  still a bunch of problems  that were still remaining in the first  version of druid we had to load data  versus  in this mapreduce process and batch  loading data was very slow  we still didn't fully have this concept  of real time  we could not explore events as they were  occurring and if we were able to do that  there were a lot of features that we  could unlock such as alerts  we could do operational monitoring and  so forth  so to talk a little bit more about how  to build  a real-time data pipeline i'm actually  going to hand things off to jion  who's going to go into a little bit more  detail  hey uh cool i think the mic works  um so like feng jin said  we sort of in the early days realized  that would make sense is to separate  data processing and data storage from  data querying  and so the data querying system that we  ended up using was druid  and like fangshin said for data  processing in the early days we used  hadoop  mapreduce which worked but it had some  issues  uh so right so it looks sort of like  this hadoop for pre-processing and  storage and druid for queries  um the way that we did things back in  those days which was maybe  two years ago is our clients uploaded  data to s3  uh we're all running our service all  runs in the amazon cloud  um and we use hadoop and pig to clean it  up transform it and join it  and by joining it um i mean that our  data is mostly ad tech data so we have  impressions and clicks and we want to  correlate those to figure out things  like click through rate um  so after running those mapreduce jobs we  load the result into druid  and the typical turnaround time for that  whole process is  two to eight hours after the event  occurs and from that point it's  available for  the sort of arbitrary slice and dice  analytics changing was talking about  um what we started doing last year was  building a real-time version of this  pipeline  um so we decided to go  go back to what the bad platform  actually does and figure out how we can  make it real time  so there's there's three things that a  data pipeline does you have to acquire  raw data somehow  you have to process that data the  transforming and cleaning and joining  and you have to load the process data  into your query engine  which in our case is druid  um so we attack each of these  three things independently the first  thing  uh acquiring raw data we use kafka for  for those not familiar with kafka it's  an open source  product from linkedin  it's uh it's sort of a big message queue  in the sky  uh you have messages coming in messages  going out and it buffers them up for you  it's very high throughput it's got a  straightforward design which was the  the reason that we chose it over the  other things out there  and what we did was we put an http api  in front of it  so our customers can post the stuff over  the web  the architecture of a kafka system looks  something like this  you have kafka brokers sitting in the  middle you have producers on the left  which are things that generate events  and you have consumers on the right  which are things that process events  the nice thing about this is that the  producers and consumers don't have to  know about each other  the producers put things into kafka and  the consumers take things out  and if one of them is having issues then  as long as the kafka brokers in the  middle are working which they usually  are everything is fine  um the next thing to tackle was  processing events to  cleaning up transforming and joining so  for that we use storm  it's a stream processor it's also open  source it was originally developed at a  little startup called backtype and then  popularized at twitter it processes one  event at a time you set up these sort of  computation graphs where tuples come in  somewhere and then fly around and  eventually  fly out and  right so we want these storm topologies  which is what storm jobs are called to  do the same thing that our hadoop jobs  had done  so we look at what the doop jobs do they  they're mapreduce jobs they do three  things they load data from somewhere in  hadoop it's usually s3  or hdfs they map the  data which is sort of taking every event  and assigning a key and a value to it  and they reduce the events which is  taking  every thing every value with the same  key collecting it together and then  doing some sort of reduction on it which  in our case is  usually a join or an aggregation  load and map operations are pretty  streaming friendly  there's not really much to do with them  you can load one event at a time and map  one event at a time  but it's assigning a keen value to it  the reduce is the tricky part  the semantics of reduce and a batch job  are collecting all values with the same  key  which presents a challenge in a  streaming environment because you don't  necessarily  know when all things with the same key  have come in i mean the key might be  a uh in our case a impression id and we  may have an impression with that id and  a click with that id  and if no one ever clicks on the ad then  the click will never come in if someone  clicks on it in  20 minutes and it'll come in after 20  minutes  so what we do and what's fairly common  is to put a window on these operations  and so that means we first see the we  first see a key of some sort  uh we start a timer and collect things  with that same key  and um eventually that timer runs out  and whatever we've got by that point we  do the reduce on  um this works it's not  perfect but it works well enough for  most applications  um the last thing to do is load  uh process data into your query engine  so for us that was druid and like  fangshin said in the early days drug  architecture was  batch ingestion based so at some point  we had to make a streaming ingestion  system for druid  and what we did was we looked at what we  had druid has  a indexing system which can take data  and create data segments  it has a serving system the historical  nodes which can take data segments and  answer queries on them  and so we just sort of slam them  together and we have these things called  real-time indexers  that can build indexes and then as  they're building them allow queries to  occur on them  um the architecture looks something like  this when you put it together  the kafka producers aren't pictured but  they're there on the left  off screen data ends up in kafka brokers  storm workers pick it up do the joining  cleaning transforming  they push things to they push events to  druid real-time workers  which are building data segments and  also allowing queries on them  and then the druid real-time workers  periodically finalize their segments  which is converting them from a right  optimized form to a read optimized form  and hand them off to historical nodes  that's really important because like  feng jin said  the data segment format is optimized  really heavily for reads  but the real-time workers cannot  directly create that format they have to  create they have to use a write  optimized format and then convert it to  read optimized later  so while that's occurring uh the druid  query broker  is able to merge results from both the  historical cluster and the real-time  workers  so a typical druid cluster will have the  past uh  one to two hours of data in the  real-time nodes and all other data going  back potentially months or years in the  historical cluster  and so if you make a query for the past  24 hours then  one or two hours of data will come from  real time and the other  22 will come from the historical nodes  um so we end up adding this third row  which is uh the real time system that we  developed  um data comes into kafka then gets  processed by storm  and then we use druid for querying and  hadoop at this point is not really in  the picture anymore  so this is what we gained um we got some  cool stuff  we got the ability for druid quarries to  reflect new events within seconds  we don't have this two-day eight-hour  delay anymore the systems are fully  decoupled which is good this means  that if for some reason there's issues  in storm then we can still accept data  in kafka so  our customers are still able to post  data to us over the web and druid  queries still work the data becomes a  little more stale but we can still query  whatever was ingested up to that point  um there are brief processing delays  during maintenance  because we need to restart storm  topologies but during that time again  query performance and availability are  not affected  but unfortunately stream processing is  not perfect  the two biggest issues that we've faced  are that it's difficult to handle  corrections  of existing data and what i mean by that  is if you have some server that had some  issue and post the data very late  it may be difficult to reintegrate that  into the cluster or if you have some  data that  is bad in some way it needs to be fixed  it can be difficult to unload the  old data and load in new data  the other issue is that the windows for  the reduces  um may be too small for fully accurate  operations  one thing that we see pretty commonly in  our data pipelines is that if we  have a window of maybe 10 or 15 minutes  we can get over 95 percent of the data  joined up properly  but that remaining five percent um gets  lost because it may take someone an hour  to click on an ad  um and uh that last little bit is  it would be nice to have it um  so hadoop is actually good at these  things  hadoop can do very big windows all you  need to do is  load all the data into a big mapreduce  job and let it go and hadoop can handle  corrections resisting data all you have  to do is rerun the same hadoop job on  the older data set  so what we can do is we can bring back  hadoop  and actually use both a batch pipeline  and a real-time pipeline  this has been described by others and  there's has been called a land  architecture so this is  our open source implementation of  lambda architecture and the way it works  at least in our case  is that we automatically do batch  reprocessing for any data older than a  few hours  and that's mostly the main reason it's  automatic as opposed to  on demand is just because we always want  to redo things with larger join windows  so when the batch job runs those  segments replace real-time segments in  druid  that's a sort of a built-in druid  feature that that makes it easier to run  this sort of system  um and the druid query broker continues  to merge results from both systems  it can take segments from the real-time  nodes um  also segments handed off from real time  to historical and also segments created  by  the batch indexer and push historical  nodes and  integrate results from all three of  those in one unified query system  the architecture looks something like  this when you put it together  so you still have kafka storm and druid  on the top path  the real time path and that's operating  in real time over on time data  and then the bottom path the batch path  operates in our case some hours later  over all your data not just out of the  time time  and that may the data that gets  processed by that path replaces data  from the  top path  um so as promised this stuff is all  open source the the cornerstones of the  stack the four big pieces are druid  storm hadoop and kafka and they all have  websites and some of them have twitters  there's also glue between them which if  you've  had to integrate open source software  before you know that  sometimes those little red arrows are  kind of a pain to develop  so luckily that's those are also open  source  um the kafka storm link storm kafka was  a community contributed module that  recently became part of the main storm  distribution  um the storm the druid thing tranquility  is something that we open sourced a few  months ago  the kafka hadoop link kamu is something  that was open sourced  at linkedin the same place kafka came  from if you're using s3 instead of hdfs  there's something from pinterest that's  similar  and the hadoop to druid link is the  hadoop druid indexer which is just part  of druid  um right so you put all this together  what you get is queries answered quickly  on fresh data  kafka is providing fast and reliable  event transport  storm and hadoop are cleaning and  preparing data for druid  druid is handling queries and managing  merging and complexity in the serving  layer  and we call this the real-time analytics  data stack mostly because we like the  acronym  we wrote a blog post about it recently  as well which goes into a little bit  more detail  thanks guys  so the question is what's the backing  storage mechanism for druid  um it's pluggable uh the two most common  are s3 and hdfs and we use s3  um  so the question is streaming in old data  as opposed to doing  batch ingestion of all data currently  that's not possible the  the the way the druid currently supports  um ingesting of old data is uh wholesale  replaces of intervals  and so you say you know this hour get  rid of the data from this hour and  put this new data in for the hour and  it's an atomic operation  um yeah so what we we actually have a  couple of pipelines so  the thing was continuous sporadic  updates of old data  we have a couple pipelines that do have  that feature and usually what we do  is we do the first cut in real time  and druid has a parameter that says how  late it will accept data  so we set that to something reasonable  usually on the order of 10 15 minutes  we do the first cut in real time and  then  we run a series of batch jobs nightly  and so the the initial cut comes in real  time and then after that there's maybe  like a  few hour delay for things like updated  um  uh the question is um do we need to know  what kinds of queries are doing before  time to get efficient queries  uh no so um  druid doesn't do any precomputation and  doesn't do any  the indexes are not customizable or  really smart in any way you get the same  indexes on all columns no matter what  um it's  it gets its speed not from being really  smart about how it indexes things but it  gets its speed from just being really  really good at scanning stuff  um and also really good at filtering  um so uh  yeah is that your question cool  okay cool thanks
 

